{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GA3fe1VdDLz7cn2YNUBfYK","type":"tool_result","content":"/bin/bash: /etc/bash.bashrc: Permission denied\nmetais render — Render the package's outputs from the inputs supplied, into a\ndirectory or to standard output.\n\nUsage:\n  metais render <package> --out <dir|-> [supply flags] [--only <path>]\n                [--overwrite] [--dry-run] [--now <time>]\n                [--sample [--optional <mode>] [--seed <n>] [--items <n>]]\n                [--json]\n\nThe package is opened and verified whole, and the inputs are supplied and\nvalidated, as metais inspect and metais validate do; an input that fails ends\nthe render with every finding of the inputs. Then every output is planned, an\nasset or a mirror as each file it writes, its path checked by the portable-path\nrules, and rendered in memory: every output is attempted, and each reports its\nfirst error. An asset is copied as it is, never rendered: its size counts\nagainst the output limits, and its bytes are read from the package as it is\nwritten. An output declaring newline: crlf then has its line endings made CR LF,\nand one naming a validate schema is parsed and validated against it, every\nfinding of every output reported. If any finding is an error, nothing is written\nanywhere. A container output is one zip of its entries, each rendered as an\noutput is, in the order its profile and its first entries give; its zip is\nwritten as it is assembled.\n\n--out is required, and names a directory. When it does not exist yet — the way\nto render — the outputs are written beneath a staging directory beside it,\n.metais-staging- followed by 16 hex digits, which is then renamed to it in one\nstep that never replaces anything: the directory either does not exist or holds\nevery output. Its missing parents are created.\n\nAn --out that exists must be a directory. One with no entry at all is rendered\ninto as it is; one that holds any entry, a hidden one included, is refused\nunless --overwrite is given. The outputs are then written beneath one staging\ndirectory inside it, .metais-staging, and moved into place one by one, in path\norder: a file whose path exists replaces it, by a rename that never writes into\nit or reads it, and a directory the render needs is moved in whole. Every other\nfile in the directory is left alone. Each file is whole, its old bytes or its\nnew, and a failure part way through lists the files already moved. Before\nanything is written, every output's path is checked in the directory: a symbolic\nlink or junction on it, a file where a directory must be, a name that differs\nonly in case from an existing one, a directory or a special file at it, another\nfile system mounted on it, or a first segment that is the staging directory's\nname refuses the render, as a staging directory left by an earlier render does.\n\n--out - writes every file to standard output as one pax archive instead, in path\norder, each entry with mode 0644, or 0755 for an executable output, owner 0 and\nthe time 1980-01-01T00:00:00Z; with --json the document then goes to standard\nerror, after the archive. Standard output carries the archive and nothing else,\nand nothing at all when the render is refused. An archive is not written to a\nterminal.\n\n--only <path> selects one file by the path --dry-run lists. The whole render\nstill runs and is checked, so --only never makes a refused render succeed; it\nchooses what is written: with --out -, the file's bytes alone, which may go to a\nterminal, and with --out <dir>, that file alone.\n\n--dry-run renders everything and checks --out as a render would, writes nothing\nanywhere, and lists every file the render would write with its size and digest,\nand, into a directory, whether it would create the file or replace it. It is\nrefused exactly when the render would be.\n\nA template sees the inputs as inputs, the package's identity as package, where\neach input came from as sources, the output being rendered as output, and the\nrender time as now, which is --now normalized to UTC, or nil without it. Nothing\nof the host — no environment variable, directory, user or clock — reaches a\ntemplate.\n\nEach output's path is a Liquid template too. An output with for_each is rendered\nonce for each element of its array, which its when, its path and its template\nsee as item, with its position as fan_out; an output, or an element, whose when\nis false writes no file.\n\n--sample renders from sample data, the author's preview: the generator makes\nevery input from its schema, as metais sample does, in the data directory's\nplace, so --data is refused with it. Values given with --set, --set-json and\n--inputs-json are kept as given and the rest is made around them, conditionals\nincluded, so one value can be pinned to see one branch. The data is validated as\nsupplied data is before anything is rendered. --optional, --seed and --items are\ntaken only with --sample. A render with --sample is an authoring command: a\ndefect of the package fails it with exit status 1, as check does.\n\nOperands:\n  <package>\n      A packed package file, or a source directory; - is refused, since a\n      package is read with random access. Required.\n\nFlags:\n  --out <dir|->\n      The directory to render into — a new one, an empty one, or with\n      --overwrite one that holds entries — or - for an archive on standard\n      output; required.\n  --data <dir>\n      The data directory: one file per input named <input>.<ext>, and one\n      directory per collection input. metais inspect lists each input's file\n      names.\n  --set <pointer>=<text>\n      Set one value at an inputs pointer, such as /vendor/name=Acme; the text is\n      coerced to the type the schema declares there. Applied after --data and\n      --inputs-json, in command-line order with --set-json. May be given more\n      than once.\n  --set-json <pointer>=<json>\n      Set one value at an inputs pointer as JSON text, the way to set null, an\n      array or an object. Applied in command-line order with --set. May be given\n      more than once.\n  --inputs-json <json>\n      A JSON object whose members are inputs, each replacing that input's whole\n      value; - reads it from standard input, once. Applied after --data, in\n      command-line order. May be given more than once.\n  --overwrite\n      Renders into an existing directory that holds entries, replacing each file\n      the render writes; nothing else in it is touched. Not with --out -.\n  --only <path>\n      Write only the file at this path, as --dry-run lists it: its bytes alone\n      with --out -.\n  --dry-run\n      List every file the render would write, with its size and digest, and\n      write nothing.\n  --now <date-time>\n      The render time, an RFC 3339 date-time with an offset, which templates see\n      as now; without it there is no time.\n  --sample\n      Render from sample data the generator makes from the inputs' schemas, in\n      the data directory's place; values given with --set, --set-json and\n      --inputs-json are kept as given, and the rest is made around them.\n  --optional <mode>\n      Which optional properties and inputs the generator makes: all of them,\n      none (and null wherever null is allowed), or a mix the seed decides.\n      Values: all, none, mixed. Default: all.\n  --seed <n>\n      The generator's seed, an unsigned 64-bit integer: the same package,\n      options, supplied values and seed give the same data. Default: 0.\n  --items <n>\n      The number of items the generator aims for in every array and collection,\n      within the schema's bounds. Default: 3.\n  --limit <name>=<value>\n      Lower one resource limit for this run, to a whole number no higher than\n      its default (a size may end in KiB, MiB or GiB). metais limits lists them.\n      May be given more than once.\n  --fail-on <severity>\n      With warning, a warning fails the command with exit status 1 as an error\n      does; no finding's severity changes. Values: error, warning. Default:\n      error.\n  --json\n      Print the result as one JSON document on standard output.\n  --color <when>\n      Colour human output: auto colours a stream that is a terminal when\n      NO_COLOR is unset or empty; always and never override NO_COLOR. Values:\n      auto, always, never. Default: auto.\n  -h, --help\n      Print help to standard output and exit 0; nothing else runs.\n\nPrints:\n  One line per file written, in path order: its size, its digest and its path,\n  separated by tabs; a container's entries are indented beneath it, in its\n  order. On a dry run the lines are headed would write. With --out -, standard\n  output is the archive, or the one file's bytes. With --json, one\n  metais.render/1 document.\n\nRefusals:\n  limit.invalid\n      A --limit names no limit, gives a malformed value, or tries to raise one.\n      Next: metais limits\n  limit.package\n      The package breaches one of its limits.\n      Next: metais limits\n  output.declaration.empty_mirror\n      A mirror renders no file, and --fail-on warning makes its warning fail the\n      render.\n      Next: metais inspect <package>\n  output.declaration.empty_name\n      A mirror would write a template whose file name is exactly .liquid with an\n      empty name.\n      Next: metais inspect <package>\n  output.destination.case_variant\n      In --out, an entry's name differs only in case or normalization from a\n      name an output's path holds.\n      Next: metais render <package> --out <dir>\n  output.destination.commit_failed\n      Moving the outputs into an existing --out failed part way; the files\n      already moved are listed, and nothing else was changed.\n      Next: metais render <package> --out <dir>\n  output.destination.link\n      In --out, an output's path runs through, or ends at, a symbolic link, a\n      junction or another reparse point, which is never followed.\n      Next: metais render <package> --out <dir>\n  output.destination.not_directory\n      --out, or a directory above it, exists and is not a directory; or, in\n      --out, a file stands where an output's path needs a directory.\n      Next: metais render <package> --out <dir>\n  output.destination.not_empty\n      --out is a directory that holds entries, and --overwrite is not given.\n      Next: metais render <package> --out <dir> --overwrite\n  output.destination.not_regular_file\n      In --out, a directory, a named pipe, a socket or a device stands at an\n      output's path.\n      Next: metais render <package> --out <dir>\n  output.destination.nothing_written\n      A write failed, another file system is mounted on an output's path, or an\n      output's path begins with the staging directory's name; whatever was\n      written is removed.\n      Next: metais render <package> --out <dir>\n  output.destination.staging_exists\n      --out holds .metais-staging, left by a killed render or in use by another;\n      nothing is written.\n      Next: metais render <package> --out <dir>\n  usage.archive_to_terminal\n      --out - is given without --only, and standard output is a terminal.\n      Next: metais render <package> --out - --only <path>\n  usage.bad_value\n      A flag's value is missing, or is not one the flag accepts, or a flag that\n      takes no value is given one.\n      Next: metais render --help\n  usage.bad_value\n      --now is not an RFC 3339 date-time with an offset, or --seed or --items is\n      not a whole number in range.\n      Next: metais render <package> --out <dir> --now 2026-01-31T09:00:00Z\n  usage.conflicting_flags\n      --inputs-json - is given twice, and standard input is read once.\n      Next: metais help inputs\n  usage.data_dir_unreadable\n      The data directory given to --data does not exist, is not a directory, or\n      cannot be read.\n      Next: metais render <package> --data <dir> --out <dir>\n  usage.flag_excluded\n      --data is given with --sample, whose generated data takes the data\n      directory's place.\n      Next: metais render <package> --sample --out <dir>\n  usage.flag_excluded\n      --overwrite is given with --out -, and an archive on standard output\n      replaces nothing.\n      Next: metais render <package> --out <dir> --overwrite\n  usage.flag_requires\n      --optional, --seed or --items is given without --sample.\n      Next: metais render <package> --sample --out <dir>\n  usage.missing_flag\n      --out is not given.\n      Next: metais render <package> --out <dir>\n  usage.missing_operand\n      The package operand is not given.\n      Next: metais render --help\n  usage.package_from_stdin\n      The package operand is -, standard input.\n      Next: metais render --help\n  usage.repeated_flag\n      A flag that may be given once is given again.\n      Next: metais render --help\n  usage.unexpected_operand\n      An operand the command does not take.\n      Next: metais render --help\n  usage.unknown_flag\n      An argument that is not a flag the command accepts, an abbreviated flag,\n      or one-letter aliases written together.\n      Next: metais render --help\n  usage.unknown_output\n      --only names no file the render writes.\n      Next: metais render <package> --out <dir> --dry-run\n\n  Each line below is a family of codes: how many there are, the cause they share\n  and their next step. 'metais help refusals render' lists every code, and help\n  --json gives each code's own cause.\n\n  collection.* (5)      An input is refused. Next: metais inspect <package>\n  csv.* (7)             An input is refused. Next: metais inspect <package>\n  input.* (52)          An input is refused. Next: metais inspect <package>\n  json.* (5)            An input is refused. Next: metais inspect <package>\n  limit.exceeded (1)    An input is refused (exceeded).\n                        Next: metais inspect <package>\n  limit.exceeded.* (2)  An output is refused. Next: metais inspect <package>\n  manifest.* (44)       The manifest breaks one of its rules.\n                        Next: metais inspect <package>\n  markdown.* (3)        An input is refused. Next: metais inspect <package>\n  output.* (34)         An output fails its validation.\n                        Next: metais inspect <package>\n  output.* (14)         An output is refused. Next: metais inspect <package>\n  package.* (47)        The package is refused. Next: metais inspect <package>\n  schema.* (10)         An input schema is refused.\n                        Next: metais inspect <package>\n  schema.* (2)          With --sample, the generated data cannot be made or does\n                        not validate. Next: metais schema <package> <name>\n  template.* (23)       A template is refused. Next: metais inspect <package>\n  template.* (9)        An output's path, when or for_each does not parse.\n                        Next: metais inspect <package>\n  yaml.* (13)           An input is refused. Next: metais inspect <package>\n\nExamples:\n  metais render report-1.0.0.metais --data data --out out\n      Renders the package's outputs from the data directory data into the new\n      directory out.\n  metais render report.metais --data data --set /vendor/name=Acme --out out\n      Renders with one value set over the data directory's.\n  metais render . --data data --out out --now 2026-10-19T09:30:00+02:00 --json\n      Renders the source directory in the working directory with a render time,\n      and prints the files as one metais.render/1 document.\n  metais render report.metais --data data --out out --dry-run\n      Lists every file the render would write into out, with its size and\n      digest, and writes nothing.\n  metais render report.metais --data data --out - --only report.md\n      Renders the whole package and writes the bytes of report.md alone to\n      standard output.\n  metais render . --sample --out preview\n      Previews the source directory's outputs from sample data.\n  metais render . --sample --set /incident/stage=final --seed 7 --out preview\n      Previews them with one value pinned and other generated values.\n  metais render report.metais --data data --out site --overwrite\n      Renders into the existing directory site, replacing the files the render\n      writes there and leaving every other one.\nmetais validate — Validate inputs against a package, and report every finding at\nonce.\n\nUsage:\n  metais validate <package> [<supply flags>] [--resolved] [--json]\n\nOpens and verifies the package as inspect does, then assembles the inputs\nobject: each file of the data directory, then each --inputs-json in command-line\norder, then each --set and --set-json in command-line order, each replacing what\ncame before at its place. Each schema default is applied to a member left\nabsent, and the whole is validated against the package's schemas and its input\nconstraints.\n\nEvery finding is reported in one run: a file that does not parse does not stop\nthe others from being read and validated. With no supply flag, nothing is\nsupplied, and every required input is reported missing.\n\nThe result lists, for every input, whether it was supplied and where its value\ncame from. --resolved adds the inputs as templates would see them, after\ncoercion and defaults, in canonical key order; in human form it is printed as\nindented JSON after the findings.\n\nOperands:\n  <package>\n      A packed package file, or a source directory; - is refused, since a\n      package is read with random access. Required.\n\nFlags:\n  --data <dir>\n      The data directory: one file per input named <input>.<ext>, and one\n      directory per collection input. metais inspect lists each input's file\n      names.\n  --set <pointer>=<text>\n      Set one value at an inputs pointer, such as /vendor/name=Acme; the text is\n      coerced to the type the schema declares there. Applied after --data and\n      --inputs-json, in command-line order with --set-json. May be given more\n      than once.\n  --set-json <pointer>=<json>\n      Set one value at an inputs pointer as JSON text, the way to set null, an\n      array or an object. Applied in command-line order with --set. May be given\n      more than once.\n  --inputs-json <json>\n      A JSON object whose members are inputs, each replacing that input's whole\n      value; - reads it from standard input, once. Applied after --data, in\n      command-line order. May be given more than once.\n  --resolved\n      Add the inputs as templates would see them: parsed and coerced, with\n      defaults applied, absent members absent.\n  --limit <name>=<value>\n      Lower one resource limit for this run, to a whole number no higher than\n      its default (a size may end in KiB, MiB or GiB). metais limits lists them.\n      May be given more than once.\n  --fail-on <severity>\n      With warning, a warning fails the command with exit status 1 as an error\n      does; no finding's severity changes. Values: error, warning. Default:\n      error.\n  --json\n      Print the result as one JSON document on standard output.\n  --color <when>\n      Colour human output: auto colours a stream that is a terminal when\n      NO_COLOR is unset or empty; always and never override NO_COLOR. Values:\n      auto, always, never. Default: auto.\n  -h, --help\n      Print help to standard output and exit 0; nothing else runs.\n\nPrints:\n  Every finding, then the summary line; with --resolved, the inputs as indented\n  JSON. With --json, one metais.validate/1 document.\n\nRefusals:\n  limit.invalid\n      A --limit names no limit, gives a malformed value, or tries to raise one.\n      Next: metais limits\n  limit.package\n      The package breaches one of its limits.\n      Next: metais limits\n  usage.bad_value\n      A flag's value is missing, or is not one the flag accepts, or a flag that\n      takes no value is given one.\n      Next: metais validate --help\n  usage.conflicting_flags\n      --inputs-json - is given twice, and standard input is read once.\n      Next: metais help inputs\n  usage.data_dir_unreadable\n      The data directory given to --data does not exist, is not a directory, or\n      cannot be read.\n      Next: metais help inputs\n  usage.missing_operand\n      The package operand is not given.\n      Next: metais validate --help\n  usage.package_from_stdin\n      The package operand is -, standard input.\n      Next: metais validate --help\n  usage.repeated_flag\n      A flag that may be given once is given again.\n      Next: metais validate --help\n  usage.unexpected_operand\n      An operand the command does not take.\n      Next: metais validate --help\n  usage.unknown_flag\n      An argument that is not a flag the command accepts, an abbreviated flag,\n      or one-letter aliases written together.\n      Next: metais validate --help\n\n  Each line below is a family of codes: how many there are, the cause they share\n  and their next step. 'metais help refusals validate' lists every code, and\n  help --json gives each code's own cause.\n\n  manifest.* (44)  The manifest breaks one of its rules.\n                   Next: metais inspect <package>\n  package.* (47)   The package is refused. Next: metais inspect <package>\n  schema.* (10)    An input schema is refused. Next: metais inspect <package>\n  template.* (9)   An output's path, when or for_each does not parse.\n                   Next: metais inspect <package>\n\nExamples:\n  metais validate report-1.0.0.metais --data data\n      Validates the inputs in the directory data.\n  metais validate report-1.0.0.metais --data data --set /vendor/name=Acme\n      Validates them with one value replaced.\n  metais validate report-1.0.0.metais --inputs-json - --json\n      Validates the inputs object read from standard input, and prints one\n      metais.validate/1 document.\n  metais validate report-1.0.0.metais --data data --resolved\n      Validates them and prints the inputs as templates would see them.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"edbb6e52-13de-4242-9ee7-038b810dad24","uuid":"5eeaf88d-7b51-44b8-9f06-28f7aed3444d","timestamp":"2026-10-21T14:10:12.303Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"/bin/bash: /etc/bash.bashrc: Permission denied\nmetais render — Render the package's outputs from the inputs supplied, into a\ndirectory or to standard output.\n\nUsage:\n  metais render <package> --out <dir|-> [supply flags] [--only <path>]\n                [--overwrite] [--dry-run] [--now <time>]\n                [--sample [--optional <mode>] [--seed <n>] [--items <n>]]\n                [--json]\n\nThe package is opened and verified whole, and the inputs are supplied and\nvalidated, as metais inspect and metais validate do; an input that fails ends\nthe render with every finding of the inputs. Then every output is planned, an\nasset or a mirror as each file it writes, its path checked by the portable-path\nrules, and rendered in memory: every output is attempted, and each reports its\nfirst error. An asset is copied as it is, never rendered: its size counts\nagainst the output limits, and its bytes are read from the package as it is\nwritten. An output declaring newline: crlf then has its line endings made CR LF,\nand one naming a validate schema is parsed and validated against it, every\nfinding of every output reported. If any finding is an error, nothing is written\nanywhere. A container output is one zip of its entries, each rendered as an\noutput is, in the order its profile and its first entries give; its zip is\nwritten as it is assembled.\n\n--out is required, and names a directory. When it does not exist yet — the way\nto render — the outputs are written beneath a staging directory beside it,\n.metais-staging- followed by 16 hex digits, which is then renamed to it in one\nstep that never replaces anything: the directory either does not exist or holds\nevery output. Its missing parents are created.\n\nAn --out that exists must be a directory. One with no entry at all is rendered\ninto as it is; one that holds any entry, a hidden one included, is refused\nunless --overwrite is given. The outputs are then written beneath one staging\ndirectory inside it, .metais-staging, and moved into place one by one, in path\norder: a file whose path exists replaces it, by a rename that never writes into\nit or reads it, and a directory the render needs is moved in whole. Every other\nfile in the directory is left alone. Each file is whole, its old bytes or its\nnew, and a failure part way through lists the files already moved. Before\nanything is written, every output's path is checked in the directory: a symbolic\nlink or junction on it, a file where a directory must be, a name that differs\nonly in case from an existing one, a directory or a special file at it, another\nfile system mounted on it, or a first segment that is the staging directory's\nname refuses the render, as a staging directory left by an earlier render does.\n\n--out - writes every file to standard output as one pax archive instead, in path\norder, each entry with mode 0644, or 0755 for an executable output, owner 0 and\nthe time 1980-01-01T00:00:00Z; with --json the document then goes to standard\nerror, after the archive. Standard output carries the archive and nothing else,\nand nothing at all when the render is refused. An archive is not written to a\nterminal.\n\n--only <path> selects one file by the path --dry-run lists. The whole render\nstill runs and is checked, so --only never makes a refused render succeed; it\nchooses what is written: with --out -, the file's bytes alone, which may go to a\nterminal, and with --out <dir>, that file alone.\n\n--dry-run renders everything and checks --out as a render would, writes nothing\nanywhere, and lists every file the render would write with its size and digest,\nand, into a directory, whether it would create the file or replace it. It is\nrefused exactly when the render would be.\n\nA template sees the inputs as inputs, the package's identity as package, where\neach input came from as sources, the output being rendered as output, and the\nrender time as now, which is --now normalized to UTC, or nil without it. Nothing\nof the host — no environment variable, directory, user or clock — reaches a\ntemplate.\n\nEach output's path is a Liquid template too. An output with for_each is rendered\nonce for each element of its array, which its when, its path and its template\nsee as item, with its position as fan_out; an output, or an element, whose when\nis false writes no file.\n\n--sample renders from sample data, the author's preview: the generator makes\nevery input from its schema, as metais sample does, in the data directory's\nplace, so --data is refused with it. Values given with --set, --set-json and\n--inputs-json are kept as given and the rest is made around them, conditionals\nincluded, so one value can be pinned to see one branch. The data is validated as\nsupplied data is before anything is rendered. --optional, --seed and --items are\ntaken only with --sample. A render with --sample is an authoring command: a\ndefect of the package fails it with exit status 1, as check does.\n\nOperands:\n  <package>\n      A packed package file, or a source directory; - is refused, since a\n      package is read with random access. Required.\n\nFlags:\n  --out <dir|->\n      The directory to render into — a new one, an empty one, or with\n      --overwrite one that holds entries — or - for an archive on standard\n      output; required.\n  --data <dir>\n      The data directory: one file per input named <input>.<ext>, and one\n      directory per collection input. metais inspect lists each input's file\n      names.\n  --set <pointer>=<text>\n      Set one value at an inputs pointer, such as /vendor/name=Acme; the text is\n      coerced to the type the schema declares there. Applied after --data and\n      --inputs-json, in command-line order with --set-json. May be given more\n      than once.\n  --set-json <pointer>=<json>\n      Set one value at an inputs pointer as JSON text, the way to set null, an\n      array or an object. Applied in command-line order with --set. May be given\n      more than once.\n  --inputs-json <json>\n      A JSON object whose members are inputs, each replacing that input's whole\n      value; - reads it from standard input, once. Applied after --data, in\n      command-line order. May be given more than once.\n  --overwrite\n      Renders into an existing directory that holds entries, replacing each file\n      the render writes; nothing else in it is touched. Not with --out -.\n  --only <path>\n      Write only the file at this path, as --dry-run lists it: its bytes alone\n      with --out -.\n  --dry-run\n      List every file the render would write, with its size and digest, and\n      write nothing.\n  --now <date-time>\n      The render time, an RFC 3339 date-time with an offset, which templates see\n      as now; without it there is no time.\n  --sample\n      Render from sample data the generator makes from the inputs' schemas, in\n      the data directory's place; values given with --set, --set-json and\n      --inputs-json are kept as given, and the rest is made around them.\n  --optional <mode>\n      Which optional properties and inputs the generator makes: all of them,\n      none (and null wherever null is allowed), or a mix the seed decides.\n      Values: all, none, mixed. Default: all.\n  --seed <n>\n      The generator's seed, an unsigned 64-bit integer: the same package,\n      options, supplied values and seed give the same data. Default: 0.\n  --items <n>\n      The number of items the generator aims for in every array and collection,\n      within the schema's bounds. Default: 3.\n  --limit <name>=<value>\n      Lower one resource limit for this run, to a whole number no higher than\n      its default (a size may end in KiB, MiB or GiB). metais limits lists them.\n      May be given more than once.\n  --fail-on <severity>\n      With warning, a warning fails the command with exit status 1 as an error\n      does; no finding's severity changes. Values: error, warning. Default:\n      error.\n  --json\n      Print the result as one JSON document on standard output.\n  --color <when>\n      Colour human output: auto colours a stream that is a terminal when\n      NO_COLOR is unset or empty; always and never override NO_COLOR. Values:\n      auto, always, never. Default: auto.\n  -h, --help\n      Print help to standard output and exit 0; nothing else runs.\n\nPrints:\n  One line per file written, in path order: its size, its digest and its path,\n  separated by tabs; a container's entries are indented beneath it, in its\n  order. On a dry run the lines are headed would write. With --out -, standard\n  output is the archive, or the one file's bytes. With --json, one\n  metais.render/1 document.\n\nRefusals:\n  limit.invalid\n      A --limit names no limit, gives a malformed value, or tries to raise one.\n      Next: metais limits\n  limit.package\n      The package breaches one of its limits.\n      Next: metais limits\n  output.declaration.empty_mirror\n      A mirror renders no file, and --fail-on warning makes its warning fail the\n      render.\n      Next: metais inspect <package>\n  output.declaration.empty_name\n      A mirror would write a template whose file name is exactly .liquid with an\n      empty name.\n      Next: metais inspect <package>\n  output.destination.case_variant\n      In --out, an entry's name differs only in case or normalization from a\n      name an output's path holds.\n      Next: metais render <package> --out <dir>\n  output.destination.commit_failed\n      Moving the outputs into an existing --out failed part way; the files\n      already moved are listed, and nothing else was changed.\n      Next: metais render <package> --out <dir>\n  output.destination.link\n      In --out, an output's path runs through, or ends at, a symbolic link, a\n      junction or another reparse point, which is never followed.\n      Next: metais render <package> --out <dir>\n  output.destination.not_directory\n      --out, or a directory above it, exists and is not a directory; or, in\n      --out, a file stands where an output's path needs a directory.\n      Next: metais render <package> --out <dir>\n  output.destination.not_empty\n      --out is a directory that holds entries, and --overwrite is not given.\n      Next: metais render <package> --out <dir> --overwrite\n  output.destination.not_regular_file\n      In --out, a directory, a named pipe, a socket or a device stands at an\n      output's path.\n      Next: metais render <package> --out <dir>\n  output.destination.nothing_written\n      A write failed, another file system is mounted on an output's path, or an\n      output's path begins with the staging directory's name; whatever was\n      written is removed.\n      Next: metais render <package> --out <dir>\n  output.destination.staging_exists\n      --out holds .metais-staging, left by a killed render or in use by another;\n      nothing is written.\n      Next: metais render <package> --out <dir>\n  usage.archive_to_terminal\n      --out - is given without --only, and standard output is a terminal.\n      Next: metais render <package> --out - --only <path>\n  usage.bad_value\n      A flag's value is missing, or is not one the flag accepts, or a flag that\n      takes no value is given one.\n      Next: metais render --help\n  usage.bad_value\n      --now is not an RFC 3339 date-time with an offset, or --seed or --items is\n      not a whole number in range.\n      Next: metais render <package> --out <dir> --now 2026-01-31T09:00:00Z\n  usage.conflicting_flags\n      --inputs-json - is given twice, and standard input is read once.\n      Next: metais help inputs\n  usage.data_dir_unreadable\n      The data directory given to --data does not exist, is not a directory, or\n      cannot be read.\n      Next: metais render <package> --data <dir> --out <dir>\n  usage.flag_excluded\n      --data is given with --sample, whose generated data takes the data\n      directory's place.\n      Next: metais render <package> --sample --out <dir>\n  usage.flag_excluded\n      --overwrite is given with --out -, and an archive on standard output\n      replaces nothing.\n      Next: metais render <package> --out <dir> --overwrite\n  usage.flag_requires\n      --optional, --seed or --items is given without --sample.\n      Next: metais render <package> --sample --out <dir>\n  usage.missing_flag\n      --out is not given.\n      Next: metais render <package> --out <dir>\n  usage.missing_operand\n      The package operand is not given.\n      Next: metais render --help\n  usage.package_from_stdin\n      The package operand is -, standard input.\n      Next: metais render --help\n  usage.repeated_flag\n      A flag that may be given once is given again.\n      Next: metais render --help\n  usage.unexpected_operand\n      An operand the command does not take.\n      Next: metais render --help\n  usage.unknown_flag\n      An argument that is not a flag the command accepts, an abbreviated flag,\n      or one-letter aliases written together.\n      Next: metais render --help\n  usage.unknown_output\n      --only names no file the render writes.\n      Next: metais render <package> --out <dir> --dry-run\n\n  Each line below is a family of codes: how many there are, the cause they share\n  and their next step. 'metais help refusals render' lists every code, and help\n  --json gives each code's own cause.\n\n  collection.* (5)      An input is refused. Next: metais inspect <package>\n  csv.* (7)             An input is refused. Next: metais inspect <package>\n  input.* (52)          An input is refused. Next: metais inspect <package>\n  json.* (5)            An input is refused. Next: metais inspect <package>\n 